    name: "Intrinsic Value via Earnings Power",
    desc: "EPV model and Owner Earnings (Buffett)",
    Icon: Zap,
    content: `**Intrinsic Value via Earnings Power** is the most important calculation in value investing — estimating what a business is truly worth.

**Two main approaches:**

**1. Earnings Power Value (EPV) — Bruce Greenwald:**
EPV = Normalized After-Tax Earnings ÷ Cost of Capital
Example: If a company earns ₹10 Cr/year consistently and WACC is 10% → EPV = ₹100 Cr

**2. Owner Earnings — Warren Buffett:**
Owner Earnings = Net Income + Depreciation − Maintenance CapEx − Working Capital Changes
This is "real cash" the business generates for its owners.

**Why it matters:**
- P/E and book value are accounting constructs; Owner Earnings are economic reality
- Buffett has used this framework to compound at 20%+ for 50 years

**Real-world use today:**
- EPV model is taught at Columbia Business School (Graham's alma mater)
- Owner Earnings is the basis of DCF analysis in every investment bank and PE fund
- Used alongside Margin of Safety: Only buy when Price < 67% of Intrinsic Value

**Key insight:** A business earning ₹10 Cr/year reliably is worth far more than one earning ₹50 Cr this year by accident.`
