    Icon: LayoutGrid,
    content: `**Diversification** — "Don't Put All Eggs in One Basket" — is one of Graham's most cited principles and the intellectual root of modern portfolio theory.

**Graham's Rule:** Hold at least 10-30 stocks across different industries. No single stock should exceed 5-10% of your portfolio.

**Why it works:**
- Reduces unsystematic (company-specific) risk to near zero
- Even if 3-4 stocks fail completely, the portfolio survives
- Forces discipline in position sizing

**Evolution into modern finance:**
- Harry Markowitz formalized this as Modern Portfolio Theory (1952 Nobel Prize)
- Led to the creation of index funds (John Bogle, Vanguard)
- Today: Risk-parity models (Ray Dalio's All-Weather Portfolio) and multi-asset strategies

**Practical framework for Indian investors:**
- Equity: Large cap (40%) + Mid cap (30%) + Small cap (20%) + International (10%)
- Asset classes: Equity + Debt + Gold + Real Estate
- Sectors: Do not allocate >20% to any single sector

**Key insight:** Diversification is the only free lunch in investing. It reduces risk without reducing expected returns.`
